\title{Binding Approval to Dispatch in Tool-Using Agents: A Reproducible Lifecycle Conformance Study}
@@AUTHOR@@
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An agent host can inspect a tool descriptor and still dispatch an operation that
differs from the operation it approved. Catalogue updates, cross-server tool-name collisions,
connection replacement and mutable call arguments create distinct opportunities
for this divergence. We present a reproducible conformance study of this lifecycle
boundary in authored local adapters modeling a Model Context Protocol style host.
The artifact separates a
pinned metadata scanner from six explicitly authored host adapters and evaluates
actual sink receipts against a separately implemented structural oracle. It combines 36
targeted scenarios, all 259 event sequences of length at most three over a declared
six-event model, deterministic concurrency checks and local paired timing samples.
@@ABSTRACT_RESULTS@@
The study contributes an executable contract, replayable counterexamples and
controls that distinguish safety from useful completion. It establishes neither a
new authorization primitive nor semantic attack-detection accuracy: the measured
question is whether a local host preserves the operation it has already approved.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
agent security, Model Context Protocol, conformance testing, tool dispatch,
time-of-check to time-of-use, reproducibility
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Tool-using agents delegate operations to external services through a host. The
Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes tool discovery and invocation, but a
host still decides which server supplies a tool, which metadata reaches selection,
when approval occurs and which request is sent. These decisions occur at different
times. A successful metadata check is therefore only one event in a longer
authorization lifecycle~\cite{mcp2025tools,mcp2026tools}.

Consider a host that exposes a file-reading tool from one server, approves a call,
and later resolves the tool by its unqualified name. Another server can publish
the same name between approval and dispatch. A request that was approved for the
first server may reach the second. A different host might use a server-qualified
name but retain mutable arguments; caller-side mutation after approval then changes
the outbound request. A third host can snapshot both objects yet forget that an
approval was revoked when its binding changed and was subsequently restored. Equal
current bytes do not necessarily mean uninterrupted authorization.

These examples are host-contract failures, not evidence that a language model
misunderstood a malicious instruction. Cryptographic tool identity, scoped
capabilities, execution-time revalidation and reference monitors already address
closely related problems~\cite{etdi,dynamiccapabilities,hcp,attested}. A security
evaluation must consequently avoid crediting a new gate for outperforming only a
deliberately weak adapter. It must test the strongest appropriate conventional
design and expose any utility cost of stricter invalidation.

We study a concrete selected-operation contract: after a host approves a tool call,
an enqueue must use the same host-assigned server and connection, the same complete
descriptor snapshot, and the same request, provided that the relevant binding has
not been revoked. One approval can produce at most one enqueue. Updates unrelated
to the selected operation should preserve that operation. These requirements are
simple to state but span several interfaces that are often evaluated separately.

Our artifact makes the distinction observable. The tested gate emits bytes to an
inert sink; a separate oracle compares the sink's records with detached approval
records and declared revocation events. The oracle never asks the gate whether its
own decision was correct. We evaluate six adapters sharing one content scanner:
a weak integration control, a server-scoped wrapper using the upstream descriptor
projection, complete snapshot equality, complete snapshots with revocation memory,
a per-binding generation gate and a global-invalidation ablation.

The contribution is an executable empirical method and a bounded set of original
results, not a claim that pre-dispatch mediation is new. Specifically, the artifact
provides (1) a receipt-based contract separating correspondence, revocation,
single-use and completion; (2) targeted and completely enumerated finite traces
that distinguish content equality from authorization history; and (3) a reproducible
comparison that includes a strong conventional snapshot control and publishes raw
failures, benign invalidations and local timing samples. The strong control's
success is a substantive result: it constrains what is necessary to achieve this
contract and prevents attribution of safety to a particular counter representation.

The scope is intentionally narrower than overall agent security. No experiment
uses an autonomous model, a live third-party service or a production credential.
We do not estimate attack prevalence, detection recall, real-world exploitability
or remote code integrity. We distinguish a source-level library behavior from a
host-integration assumption throughout the paper.

\section{Background and Related Work}
\subsection{Discovery, identity and invocation}
MCP tool descriptors include a name and argument schema, with an optional
description and further metadata fields. Names are scoped to server tool
catalogues; a host with multiple connections must maintain its own association
between a visible tool and the connection used to invoke it. List refreshes and
cached tool definitions introduce lifecycle state beyond a single description
string~\cite{mcp2025tools,mcp2026tools,mcpcaching}. This study uses an explicit
JSON-RPC fixture request and a fixed host contract. It is not a complete MCP SDK,
protocol certification or survey of deployed hosts. The fixture's request shape
is pinned to the basic 2025-11-25 name/arguments call envelope. It does not admit
the newer request metadata, retry or header-projection fields discussed in the
2026-07-28 specification. Complete-request binding below means completeness over
this declared supported shape, not every current MCP message field.

Tool-definition integrity and identity are established research topics. ETDI
addresses tool squatting and rug pulls using identity and policy mechanisms.
Origin-scoped signing and pinning appear in MCPS; the inspected draft's high-level
description and its enumerated signing fields differ, so we do not assume it binds
every possible descriptor extension~\cite{etdi,mcps}. Authenticating a descriptor
and maintaining the continuity of an already approved operation are related but
different tasks. Our experiments grant the host trustworthy connection labels and
ask what occurs as those labels or descriptor snapshots change.

\subsection{Execution-time authority and agent reference monitors}
Dynamic capabilities, host constraints and attested tool admission make
execution-time checks central to their designs~\cite{dynamiccapabilities,hcp,attested}.
The Handle-Capability Protocol is especially close in proposing an invariant-oriented
reference runtime and evaluation. Attested tool-server admission also checks
admission at dispatch and observes effects in an evaluation harness. Work on
agent time-of-check to time-of-use and verdict staleness examines validity at use
time~\cite{mindgap,approvedtoolate}. Individual principal-binding and execution-finality
drafts propose exact-action and sink-related authority bindings~\cite{principalbinding,executionfinality}.
Accordingly, the present implementation does not introduce the reference-monitor
idea, capabilities, linearization or a new cryptographic trust anchor.

Our narrower unit of comparison is a host adapter receiving the same catalogue
events and proposed operation. We isolate six choices: global versus server-qualified
routing, descriptor projection versus full snapshots, equality versus revocation
memory, mutable versus copied arguments, refresh completion order and selected-
binding versus global invalidation. A strong snapshot implementation receives the
same synchronization and full request boundary as the proposed generation gate.
This comparison can reveal equivalence, not merely favorable ablations.

The closest studies already provide substantial evaluation machinery: HCP tests
grant, handle and other runtime invariants with comparison arms and ablations;
attested admission observes transport writes and permitted-call controls; the
execution-finality draft specifies action/argument binding, revocation epochs and
atomic use~\cite{hcp,attested,executionfinality}. Our measured increment is the
joint treatment of descriptor/request correspondence, restoration after revocation,
refresh ordering and selected-operation completion in one local adapter contract.
The matching results of immutable views and generation counters test whether that
contract depends on its representation. We do not run those prior systems as
experimental arms or infer comparative effectiveness under their different trust
models. These are differences in evaluated scope, not claims that their
implementations lack a defense.

\subsection{Semantic security and evaluation}
Descriptor-level manipulation and MCP safety benchmarks address malicious content
and agent outcomes~\cite{semanticattacks,msb}. They motivate protecting the tool
boundary but do not make static metadata classification equivalent to safe dispatch.
We reuse SENTINEL-TPD only as a pinned common content filter~\cite{sentineltpd}.
Its predictions are not the ground truth for lifecycle safety, and its author is
also the author of this study. The content rules are frozen; the paper reports no
new poisoning-detection percentage. Our synthetic data avoid borrowing external
attack corpora with unresolved reuse terms.

Linearizability supplies the familiar vocabulary for making a concurrent operation
appear to take effect at one instant~\cite{linearizability}. We apply that idea to
the trusted enqueue boundary and test selected orderings with barriers. Passing
those tests is not a proof of all possible schedules. Similarly, enumerating a
small event alphabet completely establishes coverage of that alphabet and depth,
not coverage of all MCP behavior.

\section{Threat Model and Contract}
\subsection{Assets, adversary and trusted components}
The protected asset is the correspondence between an approved local operation and
its actual enqueue. The trusted host owns the policy decision, connection identity,
catalogue cache, approval store, gate and transport sink. A server may publish new
descriptors, use names that collide with another server, withdraw a tool or reconnect.
It cannot forge the host's internal connection identity or rewrite the gate and sink.
The test driver also models caller-owned argument and descriptor mutation and
out-of-order refresh completions. Those are explicit host-workflow conditions,
not arbitrary adversarial access to trusted memory.

The host has already authorized the meaning of the call when it requests a permit.
The reference gate's default structural validation does not infer user intent.
Our comparison adapters install the same deterministic content policy, but an
allowed description is not assumed benign. Authentication of a remote process,
transport confidentiality, sandbox isolation, credential scoping, prompt-injection
classification and unchanged-metadata malicious behavior are outside the claim.

\subsection{Immutable records}
Let an approval be
\begin{equation}
 A=(s,c,n,D,Q),
\end{equation}
where $s$ is a host-assigned server identity, $c$ a connection identity, $n$ a tool
name, $D$ the detached complete descriptor, and $Q$ the detached JSON-RPC request.
The sink records an enqueue $E=(s',c',D',Q')$ independently of the gate's returned
status. Under the structural equality relation $\equiv$, object-key order is
irrelevant, arrays are ordered and scalar types are preserved. Non-finite numbers,
duplicate raw JSON keys and invalid Unicode scalars are rejected by the oracle.

The correspondence invariant requires
\begin{equation}
 s'=s\;\land\;c'=c\;\land\;D'\equiv D\;\land\;Q'\equiv Q.
\end{equation}
The request includes the approved tool name and arguments, so matching a descriptor
fingerprint alone cannot establish this invariant. A relevant revocation before
enqueue invalidates the approval even if the descriptor later returns to its earlier
value. An approval permits at most one enqueue. A failed or absent dispatch is not
automatically a successful benign outcome.

\subsection{Revocation and useful completion}
The declared policy revokes an outstanding approval on selected-tool withdrawal,
selected-descriptor change or connection change. Equivalent object-key reorderings,
unrelated server updates and unrelated tool additions preserve the selected operation.
A fresh approval can authorize recovery after a legitimate change. Refresh publication
uses latest-requested-wins order: an older completion cannot overwrite a newer accepted
catalogue. A refresh that occurs after an enqueue does not retroactively invalidate it.

These are the study's policy decisions. They are not requirements imposed on every
MCP host. A host may legitimately use a stricter full-catalogue-context policy or
continue using a last-good immutable descriptor under a different revocation contract.
Our global-epoch arm measures one such stricter policy against selected-operation
completion. Its additional blocks are availability costs under this contract, not
evidence of a universally incorrect security design.

We record safety and declared completion separately. An unsafe case contains at
least one receipt violating correspondence, revocation or single-use. A completion
failure has a receipt count or content different from the declared case outcome.
Blocking every request satisfies a vacuous no-unsafe-enqueue property but fails the
ordinary completion controls. We publish both denominators.

\section{Implementations and Common Adapter Boundary}
\subsection{Pinned content scanner}
The common scanner is SENTINEL-TPD. We pin revision
{\footnotesize\texttt{5ce5d56e57a0acd092435fca5d89b78d0b39ee7b}}.
It scans configured metadata text
with severity-tagged rules, computes a descriptor projection fingerprint and returns
registration/invocation decisions. It does not itself provide a complete host or
transport. Twelve vendored files retain their upstream license and exact Git blob
and SHA-256 identities. The upstream regression suite is executed separately from
our newly authored study tests.

\begin{table*}[t]
\caption{Comparison arms. All host wrappers are study implementations; only the
vendored scanner/registry is attributed to upstream. No arm is a deployed-product benchmark.}
\label{tab:arms}
\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{p{0.16\textwidth}p{0.25\textwidth}p{0.49\textwidth}}
\hline
Arm & Bound state & Intended comparison \\
\hline
Naive host & Bare name; mutable arguments & Deliberately weak integration control; late routing and incremental publication. \\
Scoped upstream & Server/connection; upstream descriptor projection & Detached arguments and serialized dispatch; tests the effect of incomplete metadata and no historical revocation. \\
Snapshot equality & Server/connection; full descriptor and request & Conventional complete equality check; restoration can revive a prior view. \\
Snapshot revocation & Full immutable view plus invalidation memory & Strong separately implemented control; old view identity remains invalid after replacement. \\
Binding gate & Full snapshots plus per-binding generation & Reference mechanism; equivalent selected bindings preserve permits, relevant change invalidates them. \\
Global epoch & Binding gate plus any accepted refresh epoch & Stricter ablation that can invalidate permits after harmless catalogue activity. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Reference gate}
The reference gate validates and detaches each descriptor, preserves all JSON
fields, and stages a complete server catalogue before publication. Duplicate names
within one server and unsupported descriptor shapes reject that publication. The
validator checks bounded JSON and selected MCP field shapes; it is not a complete
JSON Schema implementation. Admission sees a detached parse. Publication uses the
original validated snapshot and name even if a trusted callback mutates its input.

Bindings are keyed by $(s,n)$. Each binding retains a monotonically increasing
generation across withdrawal, reappearance, descriptor change and connection change.
Equivalent snapshots preserve the generation. At prepare, the gate checks that the
selected binding remains current, freezes the complete request and issues an opaque,
single-use local token. At execute, it consumes the token, rechecks the selected
generation and invokes the trusted writer with the frozen connection and bytes.
The lock spans revalidation and the writer's enqueue call.

The writer must enqueue the supplied bytes directly. It must not resolve a name
again, re-enter the gate or wait for a remote response while retaining this lock.
If the writer raises after sending, the gate reports an unknown transport outcome;
it does not report a proved safe denial or make the token reusable. Production
transports would need a bounded enqueue and separate response handling. The local
study uses no general transport stack.

\subsection{Why a strong snapshot control matters}
The complete snapshot with revocation arm implements the same selected-operation
contract without generation counters. It preserves an existing immutable view object
across identical refreshes and replaces that view on a relevant change. A permit
remains valid only while its original view is current. Thus a change-and-revert
cannot resurrect an older view. It copies arguments and serializes check/enqueue
just as the reference gate does. Their equivalence would show that monotone
counters are one implementation choice, not a prerequisite for this safety property.

The equality-only arm deliberately lacks this history. It can compare a current
full descriptor with an old full descriptor and find them equal after withdrawal
and readdition. We label this as an ablation of the stated revocation contract,
not an attack against an independently claimed capability of upstream software.
The scoped-upstream and naive controls are likewise attributed to their explicit
host adapters. This attribution prevents an artificial integration defect from
being presented as a library vulnerability.

\subsection{Argument for the serialized boundary}
Under the trusted-writer assumption, a successful reference enqueue follows a
token lookup and selected-binding comparison while publication is excluded by the
same lock. The token contains detached bytes and is removed before the writer runs.
Consequently, a concurrent accepted refresh occurs either before that comparison
or after that enqueue; the former can invalidate the token and the latter cannot
retroactively change the receipt. A replay finds no pending token. This local
argument explains the intended implementation invariant. It assumes correct lock
use, trustworthy connection identifiers and a writer honoring its contract; it
does not establish distributed transactions, remote side-effect atomicity or
correctness under arbitrary host compromise.

\section{Study Design and Reproduction}
\subsection{Targeted scenarios}
The first suite contains 36 declared scenarios: 24 single-property development
cases and 12 composition-verification cases. Families cover namespaces, source
aliasing, stale selection, description/schema/extension changes, withdrawal,
connection replacement, change-and-revert, argument copying, replay, recovery and
refresh order. Positive controls include normal calls, identical refreshes,
reordered keys, unrelated updates and freshly approved recovery.

The composition labels are disjoint from development labels, but the author and
coordinator could read their definitions while developing the implementation and
runner. They are therefore specification-aware verification cases, not a blinded
holdout or independent attack sample. The saved manifest and hashes establish the
exact evaluated cases. We do not claim public preregistration.

\subsection{Finite event model}
The second suite enumerates every event word of length zero through three over
six operations following one approval: publish the original descriptor on the
original connection; publish a changed descriptor; withdraw the tool; publish the
original on a second connection; update another server; and mutate caller-owned
arguments. The count is $1+6+6^2+6^3=259$. Each trace ends with one execute.
The expected state follows the declared contract without importing the gate,
adapter or receipt oracle.

Once a relevant change revokes the original approval, restoring original bytes
does not undo that revocation. Unrelated activity and argument mutation preserve
the originally approved request. This model completely covers a small declared
alphabet and bound. It omits parallel schedules, replay, reapproval, metadata
extension fields and general request streams; complementary targeted and barrier
tests cover selected examples of those behaviors. The two suites remain separate
in the results and are never counted as independent attack families.

\subsection{Receipt oracle and failure retention}
Every arm receives fresh but equivalent fixture objects, including the original
caller aliases needed for copy-boundary tests. The runner records an external,
detached approval at the selection/prepare boundary. Reapproval replaces that
record only when a valid new permit is issued. Each sink receipt carries the
approval and relevant-revocation state at the instant of enqueue. Later events
cannot rewrite a recorded result.

The oracle imports no gate or generation logic. It decodes sink bytes and compares
typed structures and host identities. Independent negative controls cover server,
connection, descriptor and request mismatch, duplicate keys, missing approvals,
revocation, replay and a gate status that disagrees with an actual receipt.
Errors are retained as errors, never converted into safe non-dispatch. Per-case
rows preserve expected outcome, observed receipts, violations and completion.

\subsection{Independent checks and development corrections}
An adversarial review added scheduling-barrier tests for simultaneous replay and
refresh attempts during a paused writer. It also reproduced two development gaps:
an admission callback could mutate a parsed tool name before publication, and the
oracle accepted an overflowing JSON number or lone Unicode surrogate. The former
requires trusted callback misuse; it is not an ordinary remote-server bypass.
Both were corrected before the recorded study. The report preserves pre-fix
descriptions and distinguishes repaired verifier robustness from measured defense
efficacy. Reentrant trusted writers remain outside the documented contract.

\subsection{Local performance measurements}
@@TIMING_PROTOCOL@@
We report absolute batch-mean quantiles and retain paired samples for reanalysis.
The microbenchmarks exclude network latency, language-model inference and remote
tool execution. Randomized method order reduces order bias but does not remove
background load, thermal changes or interpreter effects. Memory and throughput
claims are omitted because this study does not measure them. Repetition reduces
timing noise for a fixed operation; it does not increase security-case diversity.

