The pinned source and each observation's code locations accompany the raw records;
the schema-denial wording appears in the scanner's module documentation~\cite{sentineltpd}.
These observations explain which obligations the study's host adapters add to the
content API. They are not additional conformance cases or evidence of MCP SDK behavior.

\subsection{Supplemental subprocess boundary check}
Six additional checks connect the reference gate to two local child processes
through standard-input/output JSON lines. The child echoes the exact received
request bytes and records its own frame count and hashes at end-of-file. Normal
calls and caller argument mutation deliver the original approved bytes; identical
tool names on two connections reach their respective processes. Descriptor and
connection replacement each produce zero received frames at both children. A
single-use check produces one frame, and a fresh approval after reconnect reaches
the new connection once. All six checks and nine transport regression tests pass.
The last full replay under Python 3.14.8 passes 84 authored and 17 upstream tests.

This supplement checks an actual process boundary beyond the in-memory sink. It
uses default structural admission and an authored, restricted call-envelope
fixture; it does not compare content detectors or establish interoperability with
an MCP SDK. It adds no cases to the 36/259 denominators and makes no timing claim.

\section{Interpretation and Engineering Implications}
The comparison separates three propositions. First, complete current descriptor
equality is insufficient for a policy that remembers relevant revocation. Second,
approved request contents and an unambiguous host identity must be preserved through
enqueue. Third, invalidating all approvals on every
catalogue refresh is stronger than the selected-operation contract and can discard
useful approvals. These propositions follow from explicit event traces, not a
claim about how frequently existing hosts encounter those traces.

A strong snapshot implementation can satisfy the same contract as per-binding
generations. The practical engineering recommendation is therefore to make the
contract explicit and test it at the transport boundary. A host can choose the
representation that fits its architecture: object identity, monotonically tracked
versions or another correctly synchronized revocation record. The artifact's
tests are intended to survive such representation changes because the oracle
does not check the implementation's internal counters.

The distinction between blocking and prevention remains essential. A status value
can say deny even after a writer enqueues and raises. Conversely, a stricter arm
can emit no unsafe receipt while denying ordinary useful work. Measuring the sink
and expected completion prevents both outcomes from being summarized as perfect
protection. The transport-error tests intentionally retain uncertainty where the
host cannot know whether a side effect occurred.

\section{Limitations and Threats to Validity}
\paragraph{Synthetic and authored scope}
The scenarios, wrappers, oracle and reference mechanism were constructed for one
declared contract. They are not an ecosystem sample or independent attack dataset.
The baseline library shares human authorship with the study. Separate code and
adversarial checks reduce some implementation bias but are not independent human
peer review. No statistical population confidence interval is meaningful for the
designed trace counts, so none is reported.

\paragraph{Conditional policy and representation}
Another host may authorize a different object or apply a different revocation
policy. Full descriptor byte snapshots conservatively distinguish values that the
structural oracle can treat as numerically equivalent, including signed zero.
They also bind unknown extension fields even if a deployment does not display them.
These choices can cause additional invalidations beyond the measured simple cases.
The serialization is restricted Python JSON, not a cross-language canonicalization
standard. The validation depth, node and size limits bound inputs but do not
constitute full schema validation or denial-of-service protection.

\paragraph{Concurrency and integration}
The finite words are serial. Barrier tests cover selected interleavings and the
gate's lock discipline; the local subprocess supplement checks a restricted wire
boundary. Neither covers all schedules or a production transport integration.
The trusted writer must obey a non-reentrant enqueue contract; long-running writers
can stall refreshes. Tokens and audit entries have no production lifecycle service,
storage quota or distributed recovery design. Fixed request IDs are fixture-only.

\paragraph{Semantic and external validity}
An unchanged descriptor can front malicious code. A correctly bound request can
still be harmful if its initial approval was wrong. The experiments do not test
LLM selection, user understanding, credential authority, transport authentication,
untrusted tool responses or sandbox escapes. Local timings do not predict deployed
latency. Results concern the pinned files and reported interpreter/hardware only.

\paragraph{Novelty boundary}
Related work already studies identity, capabilities, execution-time checks,
approval timing and reference runtimes. Our contribution is the specific executable
conformance method, controls and bounded measurements. A dated, targeted source
audit is included; it is not a proof that no similar test suite exists. A future
independent multi-host study would strengthen external validity substantially.

\section{Ethics, Harm and Data Availability}
The study uses inert local descriptors and event records. It makes no requests to
third-party targets, accesses no secrets, and executes no attack payloads. Reported
library behaviors are paired with their documented integration assumptions;
intended refresh recovery is not labeled a vulnerability. Publishing ambiguous
counterexamples without those assumptions could unfairly characterize a library
or encourage unsafe integrations, so the artifact retains controls and scope labels.

All experiment inputs are included and all study code is licensed for reuse.
No external attack corpus or commercial model account is needed. The artifact
provides vendored-source verification, exact manifests, raw JSONL outcomes,
environment records, analysis programs, tests and a reproduction entry point.
Timing measurements will vary across machines; deterministic outcome comparison
is checked separately. @@ARTIFACT_STATEMENT@@

\section{Conclusion}
Approval-to-dispatch correspondence is an observable host property with several
distinct failure modes. A content verdict alone does not specify that property.
This study supplies a replayable contract and original bounded observations across
six adapters, targeted cases, finite event words and local measurements. Its
strong comparison avoids treating one internal representation as the source of
security. The evidence supports testing identity, complete operation snapshots,
revocation history, single-use and useful completion together at the enqueue
boundary, while leaving semantic maliciousness and real deployment effectiveness
to separate evaluations.

